import . "sngl:internal/marks"

// Returns the box for `id` and `key`, starting `fetch` if no attempt has been
// made for it.
//
// This is what a data adapter is written in terms of. `fetch` is the work, `key`
// is what makes two questions the same question, and `id` names the key space so
// two adapters keying on a string never collide — spell it for the declaration
// it lives in, `"http.fetch"`.
//
// One box per (id, key), so two components asking the same thing get one box and
// one request, and a call whose key differs is a different box. The fetch starts
// on the first read rather than at construction: a key may be a loop variable or
// any other derived value, so there is nothing to hang a start on but the read
// itself.
//
// **A ref in the key is what makes the answer follow its source.** A key holds
// values, and a value keys one question forever — so what an adapter takes, and
// hands to `query` dereferenced, is the cell:
//
//     func fetch(url ref<string>) remote.Value<Result> {
//         return remote.query("http.fetch", [*url], func() => get(*url))
//     }
//
//     var a = http.fetch(url)    // the url as it is now, asked once
//     var b = http.fetch(&url)   // the url as it becomes, re-asked
//
// `&` hands over the cell rather than its contents, and the compiler reads the
// key to learn what the answer depends on: the box is re-derived when any of
// them is written, which looks up a different key, which starts a fetch. The
// caller chooses, and the two spellings differ by one character.
//
// `readonly`, not `mutates`. It depends on host state and must never fold at
// build time, but a second call with the same key returns the same box and
// starts nothing — which is the only footing on which a call evaluated during
// render is acceptable at all.
#[intrinsic("RemoteQuery", readonly)]
func query<T>(id string, key list<dyn>, fetch func() T) Value<T>
